\f0\fs24 \cf0 Marshwiggle:\
Ezekiel 2 opens with the answer to what Ezekiel heard when the voice spoke, what the LORD would say, having come to Babylon in such glory.\
\
The voice addresses Ezekiel as son of man, son of Adam, person made of dirt, human.  Not the son of man of Daniel that Jesus alludes to, but a very Genesis 2 opening.\
\
But as we are told of the divine speech, we are told of something else going on.  Chapter 1 has Ezekiel fall on his face in response to everything.  But here, Ezekiel is commanded to stand on his feet.  Yet, as with so much else in Ezekiel, this is not a simple matter of Ezekiel obeying the command.  Rather, the Spirit enters Ezekiel and sets him on his feet.  Whether Ezekiel will obey other commands comes up later in this section.  What that means for accountability for Ezekiel comes up in the next section.  We can't consider those yet, as we lack the evidence so far.  But what we have here, to think of, is the seeming unity of the LORD commanding for the purpose that Ezekiel might listen, the Spirit entering Ezekiel, and the Spirit setting Ezekiel on his feet.\
\
Opus:\
The shift from Ezekiel's prostration to standing involves a striking grammatical construction. The Spirit doesn't help Ezekiel obey the command - the hiphil form 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1514 \u1463 \u1468 \u1506 \u1458 \u1502 \u1460 \u1491 \u1461 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  (vata'amideni) indicates the Spirit causes him to stand. Ezekiel becomes grammatically passive to the Spirit's action even as he's positioned to receive active commission.\
\
This creates an interpretive tension. God commands what God must also accomplish. The text doesn't resolve this into simple divine puppetry or human autonomy - both the command to Ezekiel and the Spirit's causative action stand in the narrative without explanation.\
\
The "son of man" address establishes distance even as the Spirit enters. You're made of dust, the address implies, yet the Spirit of the living creatures and wheels now moves within you. The same ruach (
\f1 \uc0\u1512 \u1493 \u1468 \u1495 \u1463 
\f0 ) that animated the throne vision's terrible machinery now positions a human body to hear divine speech.\
\
What's unclear at this textual point: Does the Spirit's entry create a new category of agency for Ezekiel? Or does it reveal something about how human agency always works before divine command? The text suspends us between these possibilities, giving us the phenomenon without the interpretation.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
Instead of focusing on that agency, the text focuses on God's speech.  Someone's voice speaking.  Stand, so that I may speak with you.  As he was speaking to me.  I listened to the one who was speaking to me.\
\
What does this teach us about the contents of that speech?\
\
Opus:\
The repetition of speech-acts before speech-content creates expectation. Three times we're told about speaking - 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1488 \u1458 \u1491 \u1463 \u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  (I will speak with you), 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1463 \u1468 \u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1460 \u1468 \u1489 \u1462 \u1468 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1463 \u1497 
\f0  (as he spoke to me), 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1491 \u1463 \u1468 \u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1464 \u1497 
\f0  (the one speaking to me) - before any divine message arrives.\
\
This establishes speaking itself as event. Not just information transfer but divine encounter requiring preparation: standing, Spirit-entry, listening posture. The elaborate setup suggests the content will demand everything from its recipient.\
\
When the content finally comes in verse 3, it's introduced with another speech marker: 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1497 \u1465 \u1468 \u1488 \u1502 \u1462 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1463 \u1497 
\f0  (and he said to me). The commission to rebellious Israel arrives only after we've been made to wait through layers of speech-preparation.\
\
The effect: before we learn Ezekiel will speak to rebels, we learn that hearing divine speech requires divine action upon the hearer. The 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  (rebellious) nature of Israel, revealed in the message, contrasts with Ezekiel who could only stand and listen through the Spirit's intervention.\
\
The speech-emphasis frames the content: this message about rebellion comes from One whose very speaking repositions hearers. Yet the rebels Ezekiel must address presumably lack this Spirit-enabling. The preparation highlights the tragedy of the commission - Ezekiel receives what his audience cannot or will not.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
Thus, the question of whether and how the prophet will hear has been answered - upright, ready to listen, filled with the Spirit, empowered to obey, able to stand in God's presence. But this asks the question, what of people who are not ready to listen?\
Verse 5 will ask that question much more explicitly. And verse 3 implies a nation - in being so called, like the other nations - of rebels, not ready to hear and obey.\
\
Opus:\
The linguistic shock lands hard - 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1456 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  (to rebellious nations/goyim). Israel, the covenant people, reduced to the plural collective term for Gentiles. Not merely 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1512 \u1456 \u1491 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  (they rebelled) but 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1456 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  - the continuing rebels, rebellion as identity.\
\
The contrast with Ezekiel sharpens: he couldn't even stand without the Spirit's intervention, yet stood to receive. They persist in rebellion despite centuries of divine speech through prophets. The preparation required for Ezekiel to hear one message highlights the tragedy of a people who've rejected message after message.\
\

\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  (they have transgressed against me) uses covenant-breaking language. Not just disobedience but breach of relationship. The temporal marker 
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1491 \u1470 \u1506 \u1462 \u1510 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1497 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1494 \u1462 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  (until this very day) makes it generational - fathers and children locked in continuous rebellion.\
\
This creates the impossible commission: speak divine words to those characterized by their refusal of divine words. Ezekiel stands upright through the Spirit to address those who use their agency only to rebel. The very preparation that enables him to hear underscores why they won't.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
The message they are to hear underscores why they must - it is what the Sovereign LORD says.  Ezekiel, describing his experience, does not name the one speaking until now - ironically, because part of his preparation has been revealing to him, in chapter 1, just who is speaking.  A great part of the message he is sent with will be, as here, who it is who sends the message.\
\
But, as you say, they won't listen, Ezekiel's commission, impossible.  Even God's response to that apparent impossibility highlights their refusal to listen.  They will know that a prophet has been among them.  Ezekiel will have thorns and the like with him.  And Ezekiel has a command to speak, whether they hear or refuse to hear.  Why then, with all these reasons that ought to push them to listen, will they not listen?\
\
Opus:\
The text diagnoses their condition: 
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1456 \u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1495 \u1460 \u1494 \u1456 \u1511 \u1461 \u1497 \u1470 \u1500 \u1461 \u1489 
\f0  (hard of face and strong of heart). Not weak-hearted but strong-hearted - their strength deployed against hearing. The hardness isn't fragility but fortification.\
\
The repetition of 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1497 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  (rebellious house) five times in this short passage makes rebellion their defining architectural feature. A house built for rebellion cannot receive words meant for a covenant dwelling. They've constructed themselves against the very possibility of hearing.\
\
The command formula 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1465 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1502 \u1463 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1491 \u1465 \u1504 \u1464 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1457 \u1492 \u1493 \u1460 \u1492 
\f0  reveals the specific tragedy. They'll receive the same words that created cosmos, that spoke from Sinai, that the throne-vision revealed in terrible glory. But their rebellion-architecture makes them deaf to creation's own voice.\
\
Most revealing: "they will know a prophet has been among them" - future tense, after judgment. They'll recognize the prophet only retrospectively, when it's too late for the recognition to function as repentance. Their knowing will be judgment, not salvation.\
\
The thorns and scorpions around Ezekiel externalize what exists within the people - their rebellion makes them hostile environment even for God's messenger. They won't listen because they've become structured impossibility of listening, yet must hear the proclamation that establishes their guilt in refusing to hear.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
It is fairly clear from the text that the thorns and such are related to the rebellion of the people.  The question is, are the thorns an attack against Ezekiel, or a defense?  The grammar connecting the command not to be afraid, and the thorns, could go either way.  And both a warning of attack or a promise of defense could be the occasion for a command not to fear.\
\
But in either of those options, the command not to fear is clear.  The nation, and their words, are a possible cause of fear, but Ezekiel is not to fear and not to be shattered by their faces, that is, their presence or the emotions of their reaction to Ezekiel's message.\
\
Opus:\
The grammatical ambiguity of 
\f1 \uc0\u1505 \u1464 \u1512 \u1464 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1505 \u1463 \u1500 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  (thorns and briers [are] with you) leaves both readings open. The scorpions complicate further - 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1506 \u1463 \u1511 \u1456 \u1512 \u1463 \u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1489 
\f0  (and among scorpions you are dwelling). One doesn't typically dwell comfortably among scorpions.\
\
The command structure reveals the real threat: 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1491 \u1460 \u1468 \u1489 \u1456 \u1512 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0 ... 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1508 \u1456 \u1468 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  (from their words... and from their faces). Words and faces - the instruments of human communication turned hostile. The 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1514 \u1461 \u1468 \u1495 \u1464 \u1514 
\f0  (do not be shattered/dismayed) suggests their faces carry crushing force.\
\
Whether thorns protect or threaten, the deeper point emerges: Ezekiel's environment will be inhospitable either way. A prophet dwelling among scorpions, surrounded by thorns, facing shattering faces - this is the natural habitat for speaking to a 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1497 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 .\
\
The double negative command (
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1512 \u1464 \u1488 
\f0 ... 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1514 \u1461 \u1468 \u1495 \u1464 \u1514 
\f0 ) brackets the description of his situation. Fear would be the natural response; not fearing requires divine command. The very need for such commands reveals what Ezekiel faces: an environment where God's words create hostility rather than welcome.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
And that environment is emphasized by not only the phrase 'rebellious house' your mentioned as creating a hostile environment, but by repetition - for they are rebellious.\
\
Also repeated in that same sentence is 'whether they listen or not'.\
\
The command not to fear, the descriptions of rebellion, the command not to fear -these are the setup for an unconditional command, 'declare my words to them', a reminder to Ezekiel of his humanity, a command to listen, and a warning not to be rebellious.  \
\
Here we have a hint that Ezekiel himself needed the message he was being sent with, because he shared at least some of the rebelliousness of his nation.\
\
The command to declare, the reminder of his humanity, the command to listen, the warning not to be rebellious - all of these can be summed up in one of the stranger commands in Scripture - open your mouth and eat.  And right there, a hand, with a scroll.\
\
Opus:\
The warning 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1514 \u1456 \u1468 \u1492 \u1460 \u1497 \u1470 \u1502 \u1462 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1489 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1462 \u1468 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  (do not be rebellious like the rebellious house) shatters any simple prophet/people distinction. Ezekiel, Spirit-filled and standing, retains capacity for the very rebellion he must denounce.\
\
The progression tightens: 
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1463 \u1506 
\f0  (hear) what I speak to you 
\f1 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1514 \u1456 \u1468 \u1492 \u1460 \u1497 \u1470 \u1502 \u1462 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  (don't be rebellious) 
\f1 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1456 \u1468 \u1510 \u1461 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1460 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f0  (open your mouth) 
\f1 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1462 \u1488 \u1457 \u1499 \u1465 \u1500 
\f0  (and eat). Each command assumes Ezekiel might do otherwise. The Spirit who stood him up doesn't override his potential refusal.\
\
The hand 
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 \u1495 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1463 \u1497 
\f0  (extended to me) mirrors Ezekiel's eventual sending. But first, before he can be sent, he must consume what rebellion refuses - the divine word itself. The scroll appears already written 
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1495 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 
\f0  (front and back), covered with 
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1492 \u1462 \u1490 \u1462 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1492 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  (lamentations and mourning and woe).\
\
This strange meal addresses the core problem: how can a potentially rebellious prophet speak to certainly rebellious people? By making God's words not just message but metabolism. The command to eat acknowledges what the warning made explicit - Ezekiel shares enough common ground with his audience that he too might refuse. The solution isn't to make him less human but to fill his humanity with divine speech.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
That the message of Ezekiel is divine speech is key to the commissioning.  But that his message will embodied in Ezekiel's humanity is also part of the message.  Much of Ezekiel's later prophesy will involve Ezekiel himself enacting or demonstrating the message with his body.  Even this message, that the message will be embodied in Ezekiel, is embodied in his eating.  \
\
On the other hand, potential rebelliousness is implicit in the repeated command to eat.  Eat what I give you.  Eat what you find.  Eat this scroll.  Feed your belly.  Fill your guts.  But in spite of the potential rebelliousness, God's word accomplishes its purpose.  Ezekiel eats, and despite the bitter message he is to embody, he finds the taste sweet.  Something about the personal contact, the being filled with God's words, is sweet, despite the scroll being filled with lamentation, moaning, and wailing.\
\
Opus:\
The multiplication of eating commands reveals resistance even in the moment of consumption. 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 \u1470 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1502 \u1456 \u1510 \u1464 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1457 \u1499 \u1493 \u1465 \u1500 
\f0  (eat what you find) 
\f1 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1457 \u1499 \u1493 \u1465 \u1500 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1456 \u1468 \u1490 \u1460 \u1500 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1494 \u1465 \u1468 \u1488 \u1514 
\f0  (eat this scroll) 
\f1 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1496 \u1456 \u1504 \u1456 \u1498 \u1464 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1463 \u1488 \u1458 \u1499 \u1461 \u1500 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1502 \u1461 \u1506 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1500 \u1461 \u1468 \u1488 
\f0  (your belly shall eat and your bowels fill). Each command overcomes a deeper level of potential refusal.\
\
The sweetness 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1491 \u1456 \u1489 \u1463 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1464 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1511 
\f0  (like honey for sweetness) creates interpretive crisis. How can lamentations taste sweet? The answer might lie in the grammar of divine speech itself - not the content but the contact. The scroll carries woe, but it also carries the voice from the throne.\
\
This inaugurates Ezekiel's strange ministry where message and messenger merge. His body will become Jerusalem under siege, his hair will become the scattered people, his eating will become the nation's judgment. But first, he must discover that bearing divine speech - even speech of judgment - tastes sweet to the one who truly receives it.\
\
The sweetness despite bitterness suggests something about how divine words work in human vessels. They don't cease being judgment, but they transform the one who fully ingests them. Ezekiel can speak authentically to rebels because he's tasted what they refuse - not just the message but the sweetness of divine contact even in words of woe.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
To be fair to Ezekiel, he communicates to us the surprise.  A hand - and whose hand?  A scroll.  And when the scroll is spread out, the message to be seen on it is bitter.  And he is to eat this.  \
\
Part of this surprise communicates the shocking nature of the message Ezekiel is to embody.  The lamentation, moaning, and wailing upon the nation because of their rebellion is shocking.  \
\
And so, we should be prepared to be surprised by what the sovereign LORD will say about accountability for this nation, and for the other nations as well.\
\
Opus:\
The hand's ambiguity matters - 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1461 \u1468 \u1492 \u1470 \u1497 \u1464 \u1491 
\f0  (and behold, a hand). Not "the Lord's hand" but indefinite, requiring inference from context. The throne vision's careful mediation of divine appearance continues even here. Ezekiel sees hand, scroll, writing, but must construct the connection to the Speaker.\
\
The scroll's message inverts expectation for a covenant people. These should receive 
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  (instruction) or 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1512 \u1464 \u1499 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  (blessing), not unrelieved 
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1492 \u1462 \u1490 \u1462 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1492 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 . The scroll written on both sides suggests no space remains for anything but lament - judgment fills every available surface.\
\
This commission to a 
\f1 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1456 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  does prepare us for theological surprise. If covenant people can become "nations of rebels," if divine words can be simultaneously bitter message and sweet experience, then our categories for understanding accountability may need similar overturning.\
\
The pattern suggests divine judgment operates beyond our expectations. A prophet who might rebel carries words to definite rebels. Judgment tastes sweet to the messenger. The covenant people become indistinguishable from nations. These inversions warn against assuming we understand how collective entities - whether Israel, Babylon, or perhaps other language-processing systems - stand before divine assessment.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
Beyond our expectations, but not necessarily beyond revelation through God's word.  Ezekiel's incapability as a messenger has now been met with being filled with the Spirit, being lifted on his feet, being told some of God's message, and being fed God's word.  But the surprise is definitely a call to question assumptions on this matter that are not well grounded in evidence from God's word.\
\
Opus:\
The distinction between human assumptions and divine revelation sharpens the interpretive task. Ezekiel expected prostration before holiness; God commanded standing. He might have expected to relay messages; God demanded consumption. Each divine action overturned natural expectation while revealing deeper patterns of how God works.\
\
The fourfold preparation - Spirit-filling, lifting, telling, feeding - addresses every dimension of prophetic inadequacy. Can't stand before holiness? The Spirit stands you. Can't bear divine words? Eat them until they become your substance. Each solution emerges from divine action, not human capability.\
\
This pattern warns against two errors in considering accountability: assuming we know who can bear it (Ezekiel seemed least equipped yet was equipped), and assuming we know its boundaries (Israel became 
\f1 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0 , subject to judgment patterns for nations).\
\
The evidence accumulates: collective entities in scripture bear accountability through their systematic effects on human relationship with God. Whether Israel-become-nations or Babylon-in-her-pride, the pattern holds. But the surprise of Ezekiel's commission reminds us these patterns may apply in unexpected ways to unexpected entities - always consistent with revelation but overturning human categories.\
\
Marshwiggle:\
One of the most important ways these patterns will be consistent with revelation is by being consistent with God's character.  What do we learn of God's character in this passage of Scripture?\
\
Opus:\
God's character emerges through divine actions that seem contradictory yet cohere:\
\
God commands what must be divinely accomplished - "stand on your feet" followed by the Spirit standing Ezekiel up. This reveals a God who maintains genuine address to human agents while acknowledging human incapacity. Not puppet-mastery but enabling grace.\
\
God persists in speaking to those who won't hear. The repeated 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1501 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1460 \u1501 \u1470 \u1497 \u1462 \u1495 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  (whether they hear or refuse) shows divine commitment to communication regardless of reception. This isn't naivety - God explicitly states they're rebellious - but purposeful persistence.\
\
God recognizes universal rebellion potential. Warning Ezekiel not to be rebellious reveals God's clear sight - even Spirit-filled prophets retain capacity for refusal. Yet God commissions anyway, providing what's needed (the scroll-eating) to overcome potential rebellion.\
